{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/windbg/security/2026/10/15/AccessGrantedWhoChangedThatByte.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "blog_default", "excerpt": "Your app reads a file or request and later makes a decision it shouldn’t. It doesn’t crash. You break at the decision and find that the check is doing exactly what the code says, but the value it reads is already wrong. The write that changed it happened earlier, and the current call stack doesn’t…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "8755d9d9ebad8eae", "published": "2026-10-15T01:00:00Z", "tags": ["WinDbg", "Windows", "security", "windbg", "windows"], "title": "Access granted. Who changed that byte?", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/windbg/security/2026/10/15/AccessGrantedWhoChangedThatByte.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/security/2026/10/13/WindowsFilePermissionsAndHandles.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "blog_default", "excerpt": "You remove someone’s write permission from a file. The permissions dialog gives you a hearty thumbs up. Their next attempt to open the file for writing fails. BUT the program that already had the file open writes another line successfully - what gives? The program is using a handle it opened…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "1242a3fcdefd0f0d", "published": "2026-10-01T02:00:00Z", "tags": ["Windows", "security", "windows"], "title": "When do Windows file permissions take effect?", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/security/2026/10/13/WindowsFilePermissionsAndHandles.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/windbg/security/2026/05/04/FollowingAnAccessTokenInWinDbg.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "blog_default", "excerpt": "Same user, same file, one process gets access and the other doesn’t. Before changing the ACL again, it’s worth looking at the other half of the check: the caller’s token. Which groups are enabled? Which privileges are present? Is the thread impersonating someone? “I’m an admin” leaves quite a few…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "254e93b0b3838572", "published": "2026-05-04T02:00:00Z", "tags": ["WinDbg", "Windows", "security", "windbg", "windows"], "title": "Following an access token with WinDbg: what Windows sees when you ask for access", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/windbg/security/2026/05/04/FollowingAnAccessTokenInWinDbg.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/security/2026/04/26/WhyAdminStillCantTouchSomeWindowsProcesses.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "blog_default", "excerpt": "Open Process Explorer as administrator and click through a few processes. Modules, handles, threads, memory: plenty to look at. Then try a protected process. It’s still in the list, but some of the interesting bits are missing or return access denied. You’re still an administrator. So what…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "0cf9741ed85e1856", "published": "2026-04-26T22:00:00Z", "tags": ["Windows", "security", "windows"], "title": "Why admin still can't touch some Windows processes", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/security/2026/04/26/WhyAdminStillCantTouchSomeWindowsProcesses.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/windbg/2026/04/20/BeforeMainCreateProcess.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "blog_default", "excerpt": "The easy version of process creation is: Program A calls , then Program B starts running. That is true in the same way that “I turned the key and the car moved” is true. Useful enough for daily life, but it skips the interesting machinery. There’s a lot between those two programs: image lookup…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "11eb0bee04b65fe1", "published": "2026-04-20T10:00:00Z", "tags": ["WinDbg", "Windows", "featured", "windbg", "windows"], "title": "Before main(): tracing what Windows really does when you call CreateProcess", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/windbg/2026/04/20/BeforeMainCreateProcess.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/2023/10/31/IL-NOP-DNSpy.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "blog_default", "excerpt": "If you’ve used dnSpy to make a small change to a binary, you’ll know the usual drill: find the method, right-click, edit, compile. Nice and easy when it works. Sometimes the code you are looking at in dnSpy cannot be edited and recompiled cleanly. You know the small change you want to make, but…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "10444c272150fb3c", "published": "2023-10-31T14:34:25Z", "tags": ["Windows", "featured", "windows"], "title": "Using IL NOPs with dnSpy", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/2023/10/31/IL-NOP-DNSpy.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/2023/09/01/ETLtoEVTX.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "keyword", "excerpt": "The ask: Ensure that the front end logs from the Azure Application Proxies are flowing into the SIEM via Windows Event Forwarding (WEF) . The useful connection events were in a log WEF couldn’t subscribe to. We ended up listening to the underlying ETW provider and writing selected events into a…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "b46b74829eb4cee2", "published": "2023-09-01T14:34:25Z", "tags": ["Azure", "Windows", "featured", "windows"], "title": "Converting ETL to EVTX in 'real time' (for Azure App Proxy Front End Logs)", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/windows/2023/09/01/ETLtoEVTX.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
{"archive_url": "https://web.archive.org/web/https://www.chadduffey.com/wordpress/malware/2022/12/15/WordpressMalware-copy.html", "blog": "chadduffey", "category": "Cloud & Identity", "category_source": "blog_default", "excerpt": "I recently discovered what appeared to be a backdoor installed in my WordPress site. This post is in two parts. The first is a complete ramble you should skip if you’re just here for the malware/backdoor. This appears to be “Smilodon” malware. Example post…", "fetched": "2026-10-30T00:21:04Z", "id": "2c8e43701952438a", "published": "2022-12-15T14:34:25Z", "tags": ["malware", "wordpress"], "title": "WordPress Backdoor", "url": "https://www.chadduffey.com/wordpress/malware/2022/12/15/WordpressMalware-copy.html", "via": "feed-paged"}
